<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-hui-area-card"><code>hui-area-card</code></h3>

A Lovelace card. You must set `area`. Other keys:

| Key | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `name` | Replaces the area name |
| `display_type` | `picture` (default), `compact`, `icon` or `camera` |
| `color` | Accent colour for the tile icon |
| `sensor_classes` | Device classes shown as readings, for example temperature and humidity |
| `alert_classes` | Binary sensor device classes shown as alert icons when active, for example motion and moisture |
| `features` and `features_position` | Extra controls, below or inline |
| `exclude_entities` | Entities to leave out |
| `vertical` | Stacks a compact card; honoured only for the compact type |
| `aspect_ratio` | Picture shape; default 16:9 |
| `tap_action` | What a tap does; the default is nothing |
| `image_tap_action` | What a tap on the picture does; a camera view opens more information by default |

The legacy `navigation_path` key still works but is deprecated. Use `tap_action` with `navigate`.

**What the Civica theme sets.** The card surface uses the Civica square corners, 1 pixel border, no shadow, white card background in light mode and the dark surface in dark mode. The tile icon takes `state-icon-color`: ink in light mode, white in dark mode. Text uses `primary-text-color` and `secondary-text-color`.

**What stays as Home Assistant draws it.**

- The alert badge colour comes from `orange-color`, but the alert icon inside it is always white. Calculated from the theme colours, white on the light badge colour is about 5.5 to 1, and white on the dark badge colour is about 1.5 to 1. Do not rely on the alert icon alone: say the alert in words as well, for example in a reading or a neighbouring card.
- The tile icon stays round, because the theme does not set its radius.
- The text size and weight in the tile are Home Assistant’s defaults.
- Long names are cut off after two lines only when the card is compact and vertical, sits in a grid layout and its `grid_options` rows are not set to auto. Otherwise names wrap.

**What needs card-mod.** The Civica yellow focus ring around a card with a tap action comes from the theme’s card-mod settings. It needs the card-mod integration. The focus ring on the picture uses the primary colour in Home Assistant’s own style. Changing the picture focus ring needs card-mod too.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- One native card gives a room, its readings and its alerts in one place.
- It works with a keyboard and needs no extra module.
- A picture takes space, and a long row of area cards can bury the page.
- Alerts are icons, so they need words nearby.
- Alert icon colour and tile shape cannot be changed with the theme.
- A picture needs a text alternative that suits the dashboard. Check how the picture is announced on your dashboard, and say what the picture shows in the card’s text.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use one area per card and the plain name people use at home. Show readings with units. Show a reading or alert as visible words, not as an icon or colour alone. Pick the compact type when you have many areas and the picture adds nothing. Keep the tap action to navigation, so a tap on a room never operates a device. See [Cards](cards.html) for heading and image rules and [Images](images.html) for text alternatives.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content: the picture or icon needs a purpose and, where useful, a text alternative.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the name, readings and alerts belong together.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: an alert is never shown by colour alone.
- 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum) and 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: check text and the alert icon on your own dashboard.
- 1.4.4 Resize Text, 1.4.10 Reflow and 1.4.12 Text Spacing: names must still read, and long names can be cut off in a compact vertical grid card.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum): the focus cue must be visible. Check the focus cue on your dashboard.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): the tappable area is the whole card or picture.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: the picture is a button only when the card or the picture has an action, and never in the compact type.

One area, a clear name and readings that say what they measure keep the effort low.

